<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave is a Celtic burial chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt period</a> near Hochdorf an der Enz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, dating from about 530 BCE. Excavated in 1978–1979, it held a man laid on a bronze couch 2.75 metres long with eight wheels, wearing a gold-plated torc and gold plaques on his shoes. In the chamber were also a four-wheeled wagon, drinking horns and dishes enough for nine people, and a cauldron imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a> that had held about 400 litres of mead.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave.jpg" alt="Hochdorf Chieftain's Grave" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: marsupium photography · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hochdorf_Chieftain's_Grave_reconstruction.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
